# Git Workflow

## General Rules
- Git is already initialized. Never run git init unless explicitly requested.
- Use Git to maintain a clean, traceable history of meaningful project changes.
- Create local commits at logical, verified milestones without requiring separate approval for each routine commit.
- NEVER run git push, git push --force, or any other command that publishes commits to a remote. The user handles all remote publishing manually.
- Do not create pull requests or publish branches. Do not change remote configuration.

## Before Making Changes
- Run git status --short and inspect the current branch.
- Inspect relevant recent commits to understand existing message conventions.
- Review existing staged, unstaged, and untracked changes before editing.
- Never overwrite, discard, reset, stash, or include pre-existing user changes without authorization.
- Never assume pre-existing staged changes belong to the current task.

## Before Each Commit
- Review git diff and git diff --cached.
- Stage only files that belong to the intended logical change. Do not blindly use git add -A or git add . when unrelated changes may exist.
- Never commit secrets, credentials, local environment files, temporary artifacts, or unintended large binary files.
- Do not silently exclude legitimate project assets. Check existing ignore rules and repository conventions.
- Run relevant checks for the change. For documentation-only changes, at minimum run git diff --check.
- Do not claim tests or checks passed unless they were actually executed.
- Do not commit broken or incomplete changes when relevant checks fail. Attempt to resolve issues; otherwise report the blocker and leave the changes uncommitted.

## Commit Message Convention
- Follow Conventional Commits: type(scope): concise imperative description.
- Use lowercase types such as feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, build, and chore.
- Examples: docs: document project scope and workflow
- Examples: feat(home): add cinematic hero section
- Examples: feat(motion): add scroll-driven media transition
- Examples: fix(motion): clean up ScrollTrigger on unmount
- Examples: perf(media): optimize hero video delivery
- Keep each commit focused on one coherent change.
- Avoid vague messages such as update, changes, fixes, or work in progress.
- Add a commit body when it helps explain motivation or important implementation details.

## Commit Timing
- Commit after a meaningful, verified milestone, not after every tiny edit.
- Prefer small, logically independent commits over one large commit containing unrelated work.
- Documentation, project setup, features, bug fixes, and performance improvements should normally have separate commits when they are distinct changes.
- Before committing, ensure no unrelated user changes will be included.
- If there is nothing meaningful to commit, do not create an empty commit.

## After Each Commit
- Verify the commit exists locally using git log or git status.
- Report the commit hash, message, files or scope included, and checks performed.
- Report any remaining uncommitted changes clearly.
- Never imply that a commit was pushed or is available remotely.

## Safety
- Never use destructive Git commands such as git reset --hard, git clean -fd, or force operations without explicit authorization.
- Do not amend or rewrite existing commits unless explicitly requested.
- Do not commit unrelated changes simply to make the working tree clean.